A. Pusat Pemulihan Penagihan Narkotik (PUSPEN)

Q1. What is PUSPEN?
A. PUSPEN is a government drug rehabilitation facility established to provide structured treatment and rehabilitation for individuals certified as drug dependants by a Registered Medical Officer under the Drug Dependants (Treatment and Rehabilitation) Act 1983. Admission may be voluntary under Section 8(3)(a) or mandated by the court under Section 6(1)(a), with special provisions for adolescents under Section 9. Services encompass medical detoxification, psychosocial interventions, vocational and spiritual modules, and community reintegration support. The aim is to reduce dependency, build relapse-prevention capacities, and restore social functioning. PUSPEN operates within a national framework that emphasises safety, dignity, confidentiality, and the best interests of clients and their families.

Q2. How can someone voluntarily get treatment at PUSPEN?
A. Individuals may present themselves at any PUSPEN or obtain assistance via the nearest District AADK Office. Upon walk-in, officers will explain programme options, screen eligibility, and facilitate initial health and psychosocial assessments. A Registered Medical Officer’s certification is obtained to confirm dependency status, after which voluntary admission procedures and house rules are briefed to the client and family. Clients sign consent forms acknowledging programme duration, therapeutic obligations, and code of conduct. The process is designed to be accessible, confidential, and supportive of early help-seeking.

Q3. Who is eligible for PUSPEN services?
A. Eligibility covers drug addicts, drug users, and drug abusers as clinically assessed, as well as co-dependents (family members significantly affected by the drug problem) who may receive counselling, education, or family therapy. Priority is given to individuals who are medically stable for rehabilitation or suitable for supervised detoxification. For adolescents, statutory safeguards apply, including parental/guardian involvement and age-appropriate programming. Clients with severe medical or psychiatric comorbidities may be referred first for stabilisation at appropriate healthcare facilities before PUSPEN admission. All admissions are subject to safety, capacity, and suitability assessments.

Q4. Are there any admission fees at PUSPEN?
A. No admission fees are charged. Core treatment and rehabilitation services provided by PUSPEN are publicly funded. However, clients or families may bear minimal incidental costs, such as personal toiletries, optional items, or transport arrangements, where applicable. Any such ancillary costs—if relevant—will be transparently communicated during pre-admission briefing. Financial hardship does not preclude access to care.

Q5. What types of treatment and rehabilitation programmes are provided at PUSPEN?
A. PUSPEN offers comprehensive, individualised plans comprising medically supervised detoxification; physical conditioning and discipline; evidence-based psychological care (individual, group, and family counselling); values, citizenship, and patriotism education; faith-based/spiritual modules; vocational skills acquisition; and structured social and recreational activities. Peer support components are integrated to foster mutual accountability and hope. All modules are sequenced to progress from stabilisation to skill-building and, ultimately, to community reintegration. Periodic case reviews ensure interventions remain responsive to risks, needs, and strengths.

Q6. What are the eligibility and screening steps for voluntary admission?
A. Prospective clients undergo intake registration, medical screening for fitness to participate, and toxicology testing to corroborate recent use patterns. A psychosocial assessment maps triggers, family supports, legal issues, and employment/education status. The Registered Medical Officer’s certification confirms dependency under law and suitability for rehabilitation. Risk assessments cover self-harm, aggression, or withdrawal complications to ensure safe placement. If eligible, a placement date and orientation are provided.

Q7. What items should clients bring, and what items are prohibited?
A. Clients are advised to bring basic clothing, approved footwear, modest personal toiletries, necessary spectacles or aids, and relevant medical documents/prescriptions. Prohibited items typically include substances (alcohol/drugs), sharp objects, unauthorised medications, electronic gadgets (unless allowed for specific educational modules), and valuables. A property inventory is conducted at admission, and any restricted items will be safely stored or returned to family. Detailed house rules will be issued and explained upon entry. Compliance safeguards a safe and therapeutic environment.

Q8. How are family visits and communication managed?
A. Family engagement is encouraged as it improves outcomes. Visiting schedules are regulated to balance therapeutic routines and contact with loved ones; specific days/hours are notified by the centre. Phone contact or supervised communication is facilitated at designated times and in accordance with centre policy. Family counselling and education sessions are offered to strengthen understanding of addiction and relapse prevention. Any breaches of visitation rules may result in tightened supervision to protect the treatment milieu.

Q9. Are education and vocational certifications at PUSPEN formally recognised?
A. PUSPEN partners with accredited training providers and relevant government agencies to offer skills modules aligned to market needs. Where feasible, clients may receive certificates of attendance or competency from recognised bodies, supporting employability upon discharge. Participation does not guarantee employment but significantly enhances job readiness. PUSPEN also provides job-seeking skills, interview preparation, and referral to employment services. Local arrangements may vary by centre and state.

Q10. What is the discharge and aftercare process from PUSPEN?
A. Discharge is planned, with a relapse-prevention plan, community referrals, and family briefing. Clients may be linked to the Client Integration Centre (CIC) or NADA Service Centre for continued psychosocial care, vocational placement, and monitoring. Follow-up schedules are agreed upon, and any conditions set by the court (for court-ordered cases) are explained. Clients are advised on early-warning signs, coping strategies, and how to re-engage services swiftly if risks escalate. Documentation and confidentiality standards are observed throughout.

B. Community House

Q11. What services are offered at the Community House?
A. The Community House is a localised platform for guidance programmes, religious and spiritual development, drug advisory services, and community-based treatment, rehabilitation, and prevention activities. It functions as a bridge between formal services and neighbourhood supports, enabling low-threshold access to information, counselling, and group activities. Programmes are adapted to local needs and socio-cultural context. The focus is on early intervention, reducing stigma, building resilience, and promoting inclusive community participation. Collaboration with schools, employers, and civil society is common.

Q12. Who runs the activities at the Community House?
A. Activities are organised by Community House committee members in collaboration with District AADK officers, trained peer counsellors, and local community volunteers. Governance follows AADK guidelines to ensure safety, ethical practice, and alignment with national antidrug strategies. Peer leadership is encouraged to enhance credibility and relatability. Programmes may involve professionals (e.g., counsellors, religious leaders) as guest facilitators. All facilitators adhere to a code of conduct and confidentiality principles.

Q13. Who can I contact to contribute ideas or experiences to the Community House?
A. Interested individuals may contact the Chief of the District AADK overseeing the relevant Community House. Proposals should outline the intended activity, objectives, target groups, and resource requirements. Contributions—whether lived-experience sharing, skills workshops, or volunteering—are vetted for suitability and safety. Approved contributors may be oriented on safeguarding and trauma-informed approaches. Feedback mechanisms are in place to maintain programme quality.

Q14. Can I become part of the Community House Committee?
A. Yes. Local residents who demonstrate commitment to community wellbeing and agree to observe governance, confidentiality, and conflict-of-interest standards may be considered. Selection is based on community representation, integrity, and the ability to contribute constructively. Basic training will be provided, including crisis response and referral protocols. Committee members are expected to uphold non-discrimination and respect for diverse backgrounds.

Q15. Who can access Community House services?
A. Access is open to individuals experiencing drug-related issues, their families, and the wider community—including students, employers, and employees—subject to space and programme suitability. Services are inclusive and aim to reduce barriers to engagement. Where specialised or clinical care is required, Community House will coordinate referrals to AADK facilities or healthcare providers. Information materials are available in accessible formats. No person is denied on grounds of race, religion, or gender.

Q16. What are the operating hours and how are activities scheduled?
A. Operating hours and activity timetables are determined locally and publicised via notice boards, district AADK channels, or community social media. Regular sessions (e.g., weekly support groups, weekend family talks) are scheduled to maximise attendance. Certain services may require prior registration to manage capacity. Emergency or ad-hoc outreach may be arranged in response to local needs, subject to staff availability. Public holidays may affect schedules.

Q17. Are there participation fees or membership requirements?
A. Core services are generally free. Some specialised workshops or vocational activities may involve minimal costs (e.g., training materials), which will be disclosed in advance. There is no compulsory membership; however, regular participants may be invited to register basic particulars for planning and follow-up. Financial constraints should not deter participation—speak to the coordinator for assistance. Donations, if any, are voluntary and managed transparently.

C. NADA Service Centre

Q18. What is the NADA Service Centre?
A. The NADA Service Centre is a day-service facility offering assessment, counselling, psychosocial programmes, and linkages to medical care and social support for individuals with drug-related issues. It provides an accessible alternative to residential rehabilitation for those suitable for community-based care. Clients may attend scheduled sessions while maintaining family and work responsibilities. The centre also facilitates referrals to PUSPEN or CIC where indicated. All services observe confidentiality and informed consent.

Q19. What is the purpose of the NADA Service Centre?
A. Its purpose is to deliver treatment and rehabilitation in a flexible, client-centred format; offer referral and advisory services; provide care-oriented support; and conduct prevention, counselling, and targeted interventions. Programmes are evidence-informed, focusing on behaviour change, harm reduction, and recovery capital (housing, employment, social supports). Family engagement is encouraged to improve outcomes. Monitoring frameworks track participation, progress, and risk.

Q20. What is a Psychosocial Programme?
A. A psychosocial programme comprises structured interventions that promote behaviour change, relapse-prevention skills, recovery literacy, and social functioning. Modalities include cognitive-behavioural approaches, motivational interviewing, life-skills training, and family therapy. Peer support complements professional counselling to enhance credibility and hope. Programmes are dose-matched to risk and need, with clear attendance and participation expectations. Outcomes are reviewed periodically with the client.

Q21. What are the operating hours and appointment systems?
A. Standard hours are weekdays, with certain centres offering extended or weekend hours based on demand. Appointments can be made via phone, walk-in registration, or referral from AADK officers and community partners. Urgent cases may be triaged for earlier attention subject to risk assessment. Clients are advised to arrive punctually and to bring identification and any relevant medical documents. Missed appointments should be rescheduled promptly.

Q22. How is client confidentiality protected at the NADA Service Centre?
A. Confidentiality is governed by law and AADK policy. Client information is recorded in secure systems, accessible only to authorised personnel for service provision. Data sharing with external agencies occurs strictly on a need-to-know basis and with consent, except in circumstances of serious risk or where mandated by law (e.g., safeguarding). Clients are informed of privacy practices during intake. Breaches are subject to disciplinary action.

D. Client Integration Centre (CIC)

Q23. What is the CIC?
A. The Client Integration Centre is a post-rehabilitation facility for clients who have completed PUSPEN or equivalent programmes and require transitional support to reintegrate into the community. CIC provides structured residency, continued psychosocial care, and employability pathways. The environment is recovery-oriented, emphasising responsibility, routine, and community engagement. Clients remain connected to case managers and peer networks. The overarching aim is sustained recovery and social stability.

Q24. What is the purpose of the CIC?
A. CIC aims to strengthen recovery capital, reinforce self-efficacy, and improve life chances via skills training, job matching, and social support. It provides a safe, supervised setting to practise relapse-prevention strategies while re-establishing community roles. Family involvement is promoted to repair relationships and build support. CIC also liaises with employers, education providers, and community groups. Progress is tracked with clear goals and milestones.

Q25. What does “residency” in CIC mean?
A. Residency denotes living within the CIC for a defined period—typically three to six months—subject to individual plans and centre policies. Residents adhere to house rules, curfew, and programme schedules, including counselling, group work, and vocational modules. Leave and external activities are allowed under supervision or permission. Residency concludes with a planned exit and aftercare linkage. Non-residential follow-up may continue thereafter.

Q26. What is the psychosocial programme in CIC?
A. CIC’s psychosocial programme mirrors core elements used in treatment settings—behaviour change, relapse-prevention, coping and problem-solving skills, and confidence-building—tailored to post-treatment realities such as job search, finances, and relationships. Emphasis is placed on triggers management in real-world settings. Residents practise recovery routines, peer accountability, and community service. Case reviews ensure responsiveness to each resident’s evolving needs.

Q27. Who is eligible for CIC and how does referral occur?
A. Eligibility generally requires completion of PUSPEN or comparable rehabilitation, medical stability, and readiness for community reintegration. Referrals are initiated by case managers, courts (where applicable), or clients themselves, with documentation of progress and needs. An intake assessment verifies suitability and risks. Where capacity is limited, prioritisation is based on vulnerability and community support availability. All admissions respect safety and dignity.

Q28. What employment partnerships exist to support residents?
A. CIC collaborates with government skills agencies, local industries, and employers open to inclusive hiring. Residents may access apprenticeships, short courses, on-the-job training, and job-matching services. Soft-skills coaching covers CV writing, interview practice, and workplace conduct. Employment is not guaranteed but is actively facilitated. Employer engagement also helps reduce stigma and promotes sustainable reintegration.

Q29. How are families involved during CIC residency?
A. Families are invited to participate in counselling and education sessions to align expectations and strengthen support. Visitation is scheduled, and family feedback informs case planning. Residents may receive leave for important family commitments, subject to risk assessment and centre rules. Staff provide guidance on boundary-setting and relapse-sensitive communication. The family’s role is recognised as pivotal to long-term success.

E. Enforcement & Security

Q30. Will my identity be kept confidential if I report someone?
A. Yes. Reporter identity and contact details are handled in strict confidence under applicable laws and AADK policy. Information is used solely for enforcement and safeguarding purposes. Only authorised officers may access such data, and it is not disclosed to the subject of the report. Anonymous tips are accepted, though identified reports can aid swift verification. Retaliation or intimidation is taken seriously and may be referred to law enforcement.

Q31. Will the drug addict know I reported them?
A. No. AADK safeguards the identity of informants. Operational steps are conducted without revealing sources. Where legal processes require documentation, measures are taken to limit exposure to personal data. You may be contacted by officers for clarifications, but your details are not shared with the subject. Your cooperation is valuable and protected.

Q32. Can my son be admitted directly to PUSPEN after a report?
A. Admission requires due legal and clinical processes. Typically, the individual undergoes drug testing and medical assessment for certification as a dependant. For court-ordered admission under Section 6(1)(a), case preparation and court scheduling apply; timeframes may exceed two weeks depending on caseload, lab confirmation, and legal calendar. Voluntary routes are available if the individual consents and meets criteria. AADK officers will guide families on the most suitable pathway.

Q33. Why was a family member released after 14 days of remand?
A. The law limits detention while awaiting laboratory confirmation. If confirmatory results are not available within the stipulated period, release on bail may be necessary to comply with legal safeguards. Upon receipt of results, further action may be pursued by the authorities as appropriate. This framework balances due process with public safety. Families are advised to maintain contact with investigating officers for updates.

Q34. Can a person under AADK supervision be sent to PUSPEN again?
A. Not automatically. If supervision conditions are breached, AADK conducts an investigation. The court may impose penalties under Section 6(3) or vary orders based on findings, risk, and compliance history. Return to PUSPEN may be considered only if legally warranted and clinically appropriate. Each case is individually assessed with proportionality and rehabilitation goals in mind.

Q35. Is it safe for me to report drug abuse?
A. Yes. AADK prioritises reporter safety and data protection. Confidential channels are used, and operational protocols reduce exposure risks. If there is immediate danger, police assistance may be coordinated. Reporters are encouraged to provide factual, specific information to facilitate effective response. False reporting is discouraged and may be actionable.

Q36. Why is my son still using drugs after rehabilitation?
A. Addiction is a chronic, relapsing condition influenced by biological, psychological, and social factors. Relapse does not mean failure—it signals the need to adjust treatment intensity or modalities. Continued care—through CIC, NADA Service Centre, or community supports—helps consolidate gains and address triggers. Family involvement, structured routines, and employment/education pathways are protective factors. Early re-engagement after lapses improves outcomes.

Q37. Why are drug-positive individuals granted bail?
A. Bail may be considered based on health status, legal thresholds, and laboratory timelines. Courts weigh risks, due process, and individual circumstances. When results or proceedings exceed statutory limits, bail ensures compliance with legal safeguards. Conditions (e.g., supervision, reporting) may be imposed. Breaches can lead to revocation or further sanctions.

Q38. Why are addicts released instead of being sent straight to PUSPEN?
A. Lawful detention requires laboratory confirmation and judicial orders. Without timely confirmatory results or a valid order, detention beyond the legal period is not permitted. Individuals may be placed under supervision, referred for community treatment, or scheduled for court once documentation is complete. This protects rights while enabling appropriate interventions. AADK works within these parameters to balance care and compliance.

Q39. Why doesn’t AADK arrest drug dealers instead?
A. AADK’s mandate focuses on demand reduction—treatment, rehabilitation, and managing drug dependence. Drug trafficking and supply-side enforcement fall primarily under the Royal Malaysia Police (PDRM) and other enforcement bodies. Nevertheless, AADK cooperates with relevant agencies through information-sharing and joint operations where appropriate. This division of roles ensures specialised attention to both demand and supply dimensions of the drug problem.

Q40. How long will it take to get feedback after lodging a complaint?
A. Acknowledgement is generally provided within three (3) working days. Complex cases requiring verification, inter-agency collaboration, or laboratory results may take longer. Complainants may be updated on key actions, subject to confidentiality and investigative integrity. For urgent threats to life or property, immediate police engagement is advised. Please provide reachable contact details to facilitate follow-up.

Q41. Will I be informed of any action taken following my report?
A. Yes, to the extent permitted by law and operational security. You may be notified that action has been taken or that investigations are ongoing. Specific details may be limited to protect privacy and the efficacy of enforcement activities. If court proceedings ensue, public records may later reflect outcomes. Your cooperation is appreciated and contributes to safer communities.

F. Antidrugs SKUAD

Q42. What is the Antidrugs SKUAD?
A. The Antidrugs SKUAD is AADK’s community volunteer arm that mobilises citizens to conduct awareness, outreach, and support activities. It complements formal services by amplifying prevention messages and connecting at-risk individuals to help. SKUAD members serve as local advocates, event organisers, and peer supporters. Activities are guided by AADK standards and local needs. Volunteers are instrumental in reducing stigma and promoting help-seeking.

Q43. What are the roles of Antidrugs SKUAD members?
A. Members raise public awareness on drug harms; support persons and families affected by drug issues; promote available AADK services; and collaborate with schools, employers, and civic groups. They may assist in events, distribute educational materials, and conduct basic screenings or referrals under supervision. Ethical conduct and confidentiality are mandatory. Ongoing training and supervision ensure quality and safety.

Q44. How do I join the Antidrugs SKUAD?
A. Malaysian citizens aged 18 and above may apply online (e.g., via SKUAD portal or official mobile applications) or through District AADK Offices. Applicants complete registration, attend orientation, and—where required—undergo background checks. Successful candidates receive basic training on communication, safeguarding, and referral protocols. Membership is voluntary; time commitments are discussed with coordinators. Diversity and inclusion are encouraged.

Q45. What training or certification do SKUAD volunteers receive?
A. Volunteers receive structured orientation covering AADK’s mandate, drug trends, engagement skills, confidentiality, and crisis response. Refresher modules may include motivational interviewing basics, event management, and cultural competencies. Certificates of participation may be issued upon completion of prescribed training hours. Advanced roles may require additional modules. Learning is continuous and practice-oriented.

Q46. Are volunteers insured or covered during activities?
A. For official, approved activities, AADK endeavours to provide appropriate coverage and safety protocols. Volunteers must comply with risk assessments, attendance logs, and supervisory instructions. Personal insurance is advisable for non-official engagements. Any incidents must be reported promptly to coordinators. Safety of volunteers and the public is paramount.

G. Private Drug Rehabilitation Centre (PPDP)

Q47. What is a Private Drug Rehabilitation Centre (PPDP)?
A. A PPDP is a privately run facility registered under Section 16 of the Drug Dependants (Treatment and Rehabilitation) Act 1983 to provide treatment on-site or as external programmes. Registration requires compliance with standards on governance, staffing, safety, medical oversight, and client rights. PPDPs complement public services by expanding care options and geographic reach. They are monitored to ensure lawful, ethical, and quality practice. Non-compliance may result in enforcement action.

Q48. How does a PPDP apply for registration (gazetting)?
A. Operators submit a formal application to the State AADK Director with required documentation—organisational details, premises suitability, staffing qualifications, clinical governance policies, emergency procedures, and programme descriptions. Inspections and interviews may be conducted. Conditional approvals may be issued subject to remedial actions. Upon meeting all conditions, registration is granted. Ongoing compliance is assessed through periodic reviews.

Q49. How long is the PPDP registration certificate valid, and when can it be revoked?
A. Registration remains valid as long as the centre operates in full compliance with stipulated conditions and applicable laws. Certificates may be suspended or revoked if the centre fails to meet standards, compromises client safety, falsifies records, or otherwise breaches terms. Due process applies, including the right to be heard and to rectify violations where appropriate. Persistent or serious violations may attract prosecution.

Q50. What minimum standards and inspections apply to PPDPs?
A. Minimum standards typically cover premises safety, infection control, medication management, staff qualifications, client informed consent, confidentiality, grievance procedures, and emergency response. Regular inspections and audits assess adherence to policies, training, and incident reporting. Corrective action plans are enforced for deficiencies. Client feedback may be considered during reviews. Transparency and quality improvement are core expectations.

H. NADA Research Grant & Academic Applications

Q51. Can academic research be conducted at AADK drug rehabilitation centres?
A. Academic research access is limited and, where permitted, typically confined to designated facilities such as Cure & Care Clinics, subject to prior approval from AADK Headquarters. Proposals must demonstrate ethical soundness, participant protection, and operational feasibility. Researchers must comply with data-protection laws and centre protocols. Approvals may specify conditions, sites, and timeframes. Findings should be shared with AADK for policy learning.

Q52. What is the procedure to obtain approval for academic research?
A. Applicants submit an official request comprising an application letter, university confirmation, a detailed proposal and instruments (questionnaires/interview guides), and a timeline. Applications should be lodged at least one month in advance. If approved, an agreement will be executed, setting out obligations (e.g., confidentiality, reporting). If no response is received within two months, the application may be considered unsuccessful. A hardbound copy of the final report is typically required by AADK HQ.

Q53. Does AADK provide research grants?
A. Yes. AADK administers competitive research grants focusing on substance use trends, treatment innovations, psychosocial and community interventions, and service quality improvement. Proposals should articulate policy relevance, methodological rigour, and ethical safeguards. Multidisciplinary approaches and partnerships are encouraged. Grants are awarded subject to budget availability and merit.

Q54. Who may apply for an AADK Research Grant?
A. Researchers affiliated with Malaysian higher education institutions are generally eligible, subject to specific call terms. Teams should include qualified investigators with relevant expertise and ethical clearances. Early-career researchers may apply with appropriate supervision. Collaboration with AADK technical officers is welcomed for operational relevance. Prior performance on reporting and financial compliance is considered.

Q55. How does one apply for the AADK Research Grant?
A. Applicants complete the official AADK Research Grant form (downloadable from the AADK website, when available) and submit proposals to the Director General, AADK (Attention: Director, Policy, Planning and Research Division). Proposals should include background, objectives, methods, ethics, workplan, budget, and dissemination. The Steering Committee evaluates scientific merit, feasibility, and policy impact. Successful applicants will be notified of award terms and reporting schedules.